#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120653} :activity/id #uuid "288fdc58-d951-55d7-87ce-92acf93dd2ce" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048743 :topic/id #uuid "ba8c9515-59e3-55c9-9d86-1de6e55c5474" :topic/math-academy-id 1779} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121028 :tutorial/content "In the same way that we can factor numbers or polynomials, we can factor a matrix by writing it as a product of two or more matrices. In fact, there is one factorization technique that works for *all* matrices, called **LU factorization** (or **LU decomposition**).\n\nLU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/ad/adca162da15cab5989e5726acb495fc12533231c2e38b0392d68388aeb18493b.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/93/93338df4d1ee1791dc5b1679dbddff34d94d05677c362b1a41dcb925ca83a1dc.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, we can write the matrix ![](images/83/835b8ad372998cbb35be4c1998d236fe96f1d1b03ee1ad7062bdbbb77e254bf6.svg) in LU factored form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou may be wondering how we arrived at this factorization. Shortly, we'll discuss an algorithm that explains how to compute $L$ and $U$ for any $2\\times2$ matrix $A.$ But for now, let's focus on recognizing LU factored forms." :tutorial/id #uuid "827a0fea-203f-5c8c-bb6a-4d0764f6de04" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3176 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cca0ee93-f5ae-5db9-98de-167dafd23a32" :step/math-academy-id 17355 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120746}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119603 :question/id #uuid "0e45ac96-0eee-51a3-83e0-192582dcf57d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4765"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048373}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555795}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "9ff11c4f-9a18-5d14-b5c7-e64d00a7d65b" :step/math-academy-id 17356 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119793 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119604 :question/id #uuid "e1d1f80c-70d1-566d-9cc0-94899b0fa05d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4767"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555856}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "bb1d2bfc-7ab9-5e32-ba3e-2e7219f0bcc9" :step/math-academy-id 17359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119668 :question/id #uuid "ea39d5ec-9c8f-572d-8708-fdd97d0c04bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6184"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050283}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555807}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"} :step/id #uuid "ccd98e02-0c2a-5392-995e-e13e8079f540" :step/math-academy-id 23125 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120758}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120758 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121067 :tutorial/content "Let ![](images/ff/ff7b3f4bca1676cbe3fb78ae1c2a47d8fd0bc71dd98dee4c7b6fea4cc94ec7bd.svg) To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n![](images/3f/3f74fdde04340f6a4dbd86962f5a31f8af22b5fb8053c66fa3e8be41629b12a1.svg)\n\nTo find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** To get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n- we can only add a multiple of the $1$st row to the $2$nd row ($R_2:=R_2+k R_1$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." :tutorial/id #uuid "4f9a2842-c4f1-577c-827c-41e33f514c31" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3977 :tutorial/title "An Algorithm for Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "e8257db3-4571-507c-aee8-af086a0be8da" :step/math-academy-id 23628 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120654}}] :activity/title "LU Factorization of 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120001}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478487} :activity/id #uuid "02e797f8-1f67-50ff-98e9-d7a3cb69a62e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048743 :topic/id #uuid "ba8c9515-59e3-55c9-9d86-1de6e55c5474" :topic/math-academy-id 1779} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478472 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e42c85-3d13-5dc9-8c6a-51fceeb48bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477735} {:answer/id #uuid "222b38e9-e578-5db3-93ad-1ff77650be2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477801} {:answer/id #uuid "7e25a537-589e-562b-bddb-6c4194092e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477995} {:answer/id #uuid "93fc37f5-fd4a-5125-b65a-32406a3c5c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478026} {:answer/id #uuid "ad849afa-6638-5c60-a521-993d97e99b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478071}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478026} :answer-field/id #uuid "dca5f516-eb3b-5960-a63b-792e2d33df63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "17c26653-7b83-5324-906b-245647d63511" :question/math-academy-id "q-75178" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{bmatrix},$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=2,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "1c48dd59-271b-57ac-b2ac-f6dcf7b5845b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478478}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478478 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243948 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e37e6cc-fad6-5510-9620-2cbac7ce22f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241439} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba5c239-540d-5f86-ac6d-97caed8a3faf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242416} {:answer/id #uuid "8bab7e05-3097-50f9-97ae-0da4430bb726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242417} {:answer/id #uuid "8eefa27a-e9ae-567c-9c88-944a22b88b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242437} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d916f4-e1c3-59c3-b962-73fcbf193360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242604}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242416} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7fbcc62-56cc-5358-9e7d-035227d1830e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3e43d37a-2ce4-531c-9855-25d3d30a5758" :question/math-academy-id "q-106762" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 15 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 15 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-5,{u}_{22}=6,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=-30.$"} :step/id #uuid "58e0c1e2-70f8-57d5-86d1-9e1ecca5166a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478479}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478479 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ddb432-c159-51bd-87f7-46377395435b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241213} {:answer/id #uuid "797c91c1-9cd0-58dd-9630-d65ae8a82a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242251} {:answer/id #uuid "95236878-0d84-5730-9210-7d52e16f2f34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242488} {:answer/id #uuid "abfe08e7-1a8e-53cd-a0cc-09d44eab6540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242683} {:answer/id #uuid "e313faa3-97f4-5d01-bd42-efbcd8a6d8df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243200}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242251} :answer-field/id #uuid "491415e0-0ac3-5791-b266-069f3eb88034" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bd4d8bf8-4a33-530d-bc80-05a910fd04f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-106763" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -7 \\\\ -12 & 18\\end{bmatrix}$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -7 \\\\ -12 & 18\\end{bmatrix} & \\,{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5eb03b7b-af1e-525c-805a-a7fc08f84329" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478495}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399037 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba58499f-83c5-5590-b067-5c72d5b139a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399039} {:answer/id #uuid "61a41175-cafd-5d7f-88b5-4b344125cf85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399040} {:answer/id #uuid "991289dd-6e89-58ad-98ec-431b03eb6134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399041} {:answer/id #uuid "46a45cff-fcc3-53f3-b793-ae6574c91421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399042} {:answer/id #uuid "ff494868-b717-56d0-b9b8-ba8b8cadd615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399043}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399039} :answer-field/id #uuid "63a6c2d7-95e4-53da-98cc-2de47ce76700" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "30a5731c-2a02-53f9-9c62-2c24fdfa7142" :question/math-academy-id "q-75177" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-\\frac{1}{2},$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "b49cc272-671e-59d4-b2ca-4c2b37016619" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478472}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478495 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55a4dac3-2c33-5d58-aaa2-125e694888fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477913} {:answer/id #uuid "6500c57c-c609-50e3-acfa-96edb3d0cc4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477941} {:answer/id #uuid "de08e829-7d86-5d17-a65c-17f17f9ad7cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478170} {:answer/id #uuid "f44e7b1e-79e3-5325-be32-3303f3918e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478218} {:answer/id #uuid "fe1b79e5-9189-58f5-bacb-bfc122c496e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478242}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478242} :answer-field/id #uuid "d178ac13-c211-5fc3-94f5-4e3a59d84b42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7301bd89-49bc-52af-80e0-064f8b80e2ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-106770" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 9 & -15\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 9 & -15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-3,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-3.$"} :step/id #uuid "ee4afbcd-af83-5653-a77b-3dd934feb1aa"}] :activity/title "LU Factorization of 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477727}]}
